        ":question/id": "dfd0ffab-79f2-5643-9530-8f55bce579c3",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-101417",
        ":question/problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-2)+2x\\cdot x \\\\  & =-18+2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -18+2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-3.$"
      },
